==============================================================================
 PHASE 1 NOTES - SOURCE INVENTORY  (simple language)
==============================================================================

Goal of Phase 1:
  Teach CodePulse to LOOK at a folder and LIST the files in it.
  It does NOT read Java code yet. Like taking attendance before class:
  "who is here, and how big are they?"

Why a separate step?
  Later (Phase 7) the folder will come from a GitHub download. Later
  still (Phase 2) we will parse the Java files. Keeping "find the files"
  apart from "understand the files" means each part can be tested alone.

Slices:
  1A  Walk a folder, skip junk folders, sorted list with sizes   <- DONE
  1B  Count lines safely (size cap, Windows line endings)     <- DONE
  1C  Label files MAIN / TEST / OTHER, add the workspace interface


------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 SLICE 1A - NEW IDEAS
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

1. Path  = an ADDRESS of a file or folder. Just the address, not the file.
     Path p = Path.of("src/main/java/App.java");
   Creating a Path does not touch the disk. The file might not even exist.

2. Files = a toolbox of static methods that DO touch the disk.
     Files.exists(p)      is it there?
     Files.size(p)        how many bytes?
     Files.isRegularFile  a normal file (not a folder, not a shortcut)?
     Files.walk(folder)   visit everything inside, all levels deep

3. try-with-resources = "always close the door, even if you trip".
     try (Stream<Path> paths = Files.walk(base)) {
         ... use paths ...
     }   <- Java calls paths.close() HERE, always.
   Files.walk borrows "directory handles" from the operating system.
   The OS only has a limited number to lend. If you forget to return
   them, a long-running server eventually cannot open any file at all.
   try-with-resources returns them even when an exception happens in
   the middle. That is the Phase 1 gate question.

4. Checked exceptions (IOException).
   Disk work can fail for reasons outside your code: folder deleted,
   no permission. Java FORCES you to either handle IOException or
   write "throws IOException" on the method. That is why scan() says
   "throws IOException".
   Side effect: a lambda inside forEach(...) is not allowed to throw a
   checked exception. So SourceInventory uses a plain while-loop with
   an Iterator instead. Simple and honest.

5. UncheckedIOException.
   The stream's iterator cannot throw IOException, so Java wraps it
   in UncheckedIOException. We catch it and throw the real one inside
   ("unwrap"), so callers see the true cause.

6. Symbolic links (shortcuts).
   A symlink is a file that POINTS somewhere else, maybe outside the
   folder, maybe at /etc/passwd. An analyzer that follows links could
   be tricked into reading files it should never see. So we use
   LinkOption.NOFOLLOW_LINKS and never list or follow them.

7. Relative POSIX paths.
   Mac says  src/main/App.java   Windows says  src\main\App.java
   We always store "src/main/App.java" relative to the scanned folder.
   Same text on every computer = same report. And we never store the
   full server path like /Users/aditya/..., which would leak private
   details.

8. Sorting with a Comparator.
     files.sort(Comparator.comparing(SourceFile::relativePath));
   "Sort files by their relativePath." Files.walk returns files in
   whatever order the disk likes, so we always sort afterwards.
   Note: uppercase comes before lowercase ("Target" < "builder").

9. Excluded folders are COUNTED, not hidden.
   .git, node_modules, vendor, target, build, generated, generated-sources
   Files inside them go into excludedFileCount. Honest reports never
   let things quietly disappear.

10. @TempDir (JUnit).
   JUnit gives each test a fresh empty folder and deletes it after.
   Tests build tiny fake projects there. Your real files are never
   touched.


------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 SLICE 1A - HOW IT FLOWS
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  scan(root)
    -> make root absolute and clean       ( /.../backend )
    -> missing?  throw NoSuchFileException
    -> not a folder or a symlink?  throw NotDirectoryException
    -> open Files.walk inside try-with-resources
         for each path:
           not a regular file?           skip (folders, symlinks)
           inside an excluded folder?    excluded++ , skip
           otherwise                     add SourceFile(path, size)
    -> stream closed automatically
    -> sort by path
    -> return InventoryResult(files, excludedCount)


------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 HOW TO TEST SLICE 1A, STEP BY STEP
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

STEP 1  cd ~/Desktop/CodePulse/backend
STEP 2  ./mvnw test
        Expect:  Tests run: 15, Failures: 0, Errors: 0   BUILD SUCCESS
        (8 of those 15 are the new SourceInventoryTest)

STEP 3  Try it on a real folder, the backend project itself:
          java -cp target/classes dev.codepulse.InventoryMain .
        Output: one line per file ("path  N bytes"), then
          included files: 14
          excluded files: 22
        The 22 excluded files are the compiled .class files and test
        reports inside target/. They were found and counted, but kept
        out of the list, exactly as designed.

STEP 4  Try a folder that does not exist:
          java -cp target/classes dev.codepulse.InventoryMain nope
        You get a NoSuchFileException stack trace. Read it top to bottom
        like in Phase 0.

What each test proves:
  emptyDirectoryHasNoFiles                      empty folder = empty result
  listsNestedFilesSortedWithForwardSlashPaths   sorted, uses "/"
  recordsExactByteSizes                         sizes are exact bytes
  skipsExcludedDirectoriesButCountsTheirFiles   junk folders counted, not listed
  excludesOnlyExactDirectoryNames...            "builder" is not "build"
  doesNotFollowOrListSymbolicLinks              shortcuts ignored (security)
  rejectsMissingRoot                            missing folder = clear error
  rejectsRootThatIsAFile                        a file is not a folder


==============================================================================
 SLICE 1B - COUNTING LINES SAFELY
==============================================================================

What it does:
  For every .java file, count its lines. Other files (README, pom.xml,
  images) are NOT counted; their line count is null.

The rules (Blueprint 9.3, "physicalLoc"):
  ""                  -> 0 lines   (empty file)
  "class A {}"        -> 1 line
  "class A {}\n"      -> 1 line   (a final newline does NOT add a line)
  "a\n\nb\n"          -> 3 lines  (the empty middle line still counts)
  "a\r\nb\r\n"        -> 2 lines  (Windows \r\n = ONE line break)
  "a\rb"              -> 2 lines  (old-Mac \r alone = one line break)

Line endings, in one picture:
  Mac/Linux  ends a line with   \n       (1 byte)
  Windows    ends a line with   \r\n     (2 bytes)
  If we counted every \r and every \n, Windows files would look twice
  as long. So when \n comes right after \r, we don't count it again.

NEW IDEA 1 - null means "not measured", 0 means "measured and empty".
  SourceFile.physicalLines is an Integer (can be null), not an int.
  README.md -> null  (we did not look)
  Empty.java -> 0    (we looked, it is empty)
  Writing 0 for README would be a lie. CodePulse never fakes a number.

NEW IDEA 2 - bounded reading (never trust a file's size).
  A .java file bigger than 256 KiB stops the whole scan with
  FileSizeLimitExceededException. We check twice:
    a) Files.size() first - cheap, catches most big files.
    b) While reading, we stop the moment we pass the limit.
  Why twice? A file can grow between (a) and (b). Untrusted input must
  never make us read an unlimited amount of data.

NEW IDEA 3 - reading in chunks (buffer).
  We read 8192 bytes at a time, not the whole file at once. Tricky case:
  the \r lands at the END of one chunk and the \n at the START of the
  next. A flag (previousWasCarriageReturn) remembers the \r across
  chunks. Test: crlfSplitAcrossTheReadBufferIsStillOneLineBreak.

NEW IDEA 4 - bytes vs characters (UTF-8).
  "é" is 1 character but 2 bytes. "日" is 1 character but 3 bytes.
  sizeBytes counts BYTES. Line counting also works on bytes, which is
  safe because the bytes for \n and \r never appear inside a
  multi-byte character.

NEW IDEA 5 - our own exception class.
  FileSizeLimitExceededException extends IOException (checked), because
  it is about the INPUT, not a bug in our code, and the caller must
  decide how to report it. It carries the path and the limit.


------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 DEBUGGING LESSON FROM SLICE 1B (real, 2026-09-23)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

First run: 2 tests FAILED.
  expected: <22> but was: <21>
  ... sizeBytes=13 ... but was ... sizeBytes=14 ...

Hypothesis: the code is right; the hand-counted EXPECTED values in the
tests were wrong (every line-count check had passed; only sizes failed).
Evidence: counted the same strings independently with Python:
  "class App {\n}\n" = 14 bytes,  UTF-8 text = 21 chars / 26 bytes.
Fix: corrected the expected numbers, wrote the counting in comments.

Lesson: when a test fails, first ask "is the code wrong, or is the
TEST wrong?" Find evidence OUTSIDE the code before changing anything.
Never just copy whatever the code printed into the test - then the
test proves nothing.


------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 HOW TO TEST SLICE 1B
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

STEP 1  cd ~/Desktop/CodePulse/backend
STEP 2  ./mvnw test
        Expect: Tests run: 30, Failures: 0, Errors: 0   BUILD SUCCESS
        New: PhysicalLineCounterTest (11), SourceInventoryTest now 12.

STEP 3  java -cp target/classes dev.codepulse.InventoryMain .
        Now each line looks like:
          pom.xml  2294 bytes  lines not measured
          src/main/java/dev/codepulse/Phase0Main.java  1807 bytes  44 lines
        The .java files get a line count; everything else says
        "lines not measured".

STEP 4  Compare with the Mac's own line counter:
          wc -l src/main/java/dev/codepulse/Phase0Main.java
        It also says 44. (wc -l counts \n characters, so it only agrees
        when the file ends with a newline - ours all do.)
